  "issue_number": 15723,
  "issue_title": "compiler-core: v-memo + v-for cache guard is not parenthesised, so a ternary :key crashes at render",
  "issue_body": "### Vue version\n\n3.5.43\n\n### Link to minimal reproduction\n\nhttps://play.vuejs.org/#\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n\n1. Open the reproduction link.\n2. Open the browser console. The component throws on the very first render and never mounts.\n\nThis also reproduces outside the browser via the compiler alone:\n\n```js\nconst { compileTemplate } = require('@vue/compiler-sfc');\ncompileTemplate({\n  id: 'x',\n  filename: 'x.vue',\n  source: `<span v-for=\"item in items\" :key=\"item.kind === 'a' ? item.a : item.b\" v-memo=\"[item]\">{{ item.a }}</span>`,\n});\n```\n\nThe generated guard inserts the `:key` without parentheses:\n\n```js\nif (_cached && _cached.el && _cached.key === item.kind === 'a' ? item.a : item.b && _isMemoSame(_cached, _memo)) return _cached\n```\n\n### What is expected?\n\nThe component renders `x`.\n\nThe same component renders correctly when the `:key` is a higher-precedence expression, e.g. `:key=\"item.a\"` or `:key=\"getKey(item)\"`.\n\n\n### What is actually happening?\n\nIt throws during render (on the first render, so it also fails under SSR):\n\n```\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'memo')\n    at isMemoSame (@vue/runtime-core/dist/runtime-core.cjs.js:...)\n```\n\n\n### System Info\n\n```shell\nSystem:\n    OS: macOS 27.0\n    CPU: (14) arm64 Apple M4 Pro\n    Memory: 77.08 MB / 24.00 GB\n    Shell: 5.9 - /bin/zsh\n  Binaries:\n    Node: 26.10.0 - /opt/homebrew/bin/node\n    npm: 11.19.1 - /opt/homebrew/bin/npm\n    Deno: 2.9.7 - /opt/homebrew/bin/deno\n  Browsers:\n    Chrome: 154.0.8037.92\n    Firefox Developer Edition: 157.0\n    Safari: 27.0\n  npmPackages:\n    vue: 3.5.43 => 3.5.43\n```\n\n### Any additional comments?\n\nI encountered this bug working on one of my projects. The analysis and root cause below have been worked out by AI. However, I confirm I've checked the reproduction and details above are correct.\n\n### Root cause\n\nIn `packages/compiler-core/src/transforms/vFor.ts`, the `v-memo` + `v-for` loop builder emits the item's `:key` expression directly into the cache guard without wrapping it in parentheses:\n\n```ts\nloop.body = createBlockStatement([\n  createCompoundExpression([`const _memo = (`, memo.exp, `)`]),\n  createCompoundExpression([\n    `if (_cached && _cached.el`,\n    ...(keyExp ? [` && _cached.key === `, keyExp] : []),\n    ` && ${context.helperString(IS_MEMO_SAME)}(_cached, _memo)) return _cached`,\n  ]),\n  // ...\n]);\n```\n\nBecause `?:` has **lower precedence** than `&&`, a ternary key makes the generated condition parse as:\n\n```js\n(_cached && _cached.el && ((_cached.key === item.kind) === 'a'))\n  ? item.a\n  : (item.b && _isMemoSame(_cached, _memo))\n```\n\nThe `_isMemoSame(...)` call therefore falls into the **else branch of the ternary** instead of being part of the guard. On the first render `_cached` is `undefined` (the `_cache` slot is empty), the condition is falsy, and `_isMemoSame(undefined, _memo)` is invoked \u2014 `isMemoSame` then reads `cached.memo` on `undefined`.\n\nAny low-precedence `:key` expression triggers this: ternary (`a ? b : c`), logical (`a || b`, `a ?? b`), assignment, etc. Only higher-precedence keys (identifiers, member expressions, call expressions) happen to compile to a correctly-parsed guard \u2014 which is why the [documented example](https://vuejs.org/api/built-in-directives.html#v-memo) (`:key=\"item.id\"`) works and this slipped through.\n\nThe [docs](https://vuejs.org/api/built-in-directives.html#v-memo) only require `v-memo` and `v-for` to be used on the same element (they are, here) and place no restriction on the `:key` expression, so this is within the documented usage.\n\nThis appears to be a sibling of [#14859](https://github.com/vuejs/core/issues/14859) (the `<template v-for>` + non-identifier key + `v-memo` compile-time crash); both stem from the eager key handling added in [#12014](https://github.com/vuejs/core/pull/12014) (commit `99009ee`, released in 3.5.13 \u2014 so this is likely present from 3.5.13 onward, and the offending code is still on `main`).\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nWrap the key expression when generating the guard:\n\n```ts\n...(keyExp ? [` && _cached.key === (`, keyExp, `)`] : []),\n```\n\nwhich yields:\n\n```js\n_cached && _cached.el && _cached.key === (item.kind === 'a' ? item.a : item.b) && _isMemoSame(_cached, _memo)\n```\n\nA compiler snapshot test for `v-for` + `v-memo` + a ternary `:key`, plus a runtime test that mounts without throwing, would cover the regression.\n\n### Workaround\n\nMove the key expression into a function or member expression so the emitted guard has the correct precedence, e.g. `:key=\"rowKey(row)\"`.\n",
